---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import GreenfieldDiagram from "../components/GreenfieldDiagram.astro";
import DiagramIdentity from "../components/DiagramIdentity.astro";

const heroTitle = "I like building systems.";
const heroSubtitle =
  "I'd like to think I build systems to reconnect with the world; in reality, I just like making apps and data work together.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Emmanuel Quagraine | Portfolio"
  description="Event-driven architect and data platform engineer focused on unlocking clarity from complex telemetry."
  siteName="Emmanuel Quagraine"
>
  <section class="mb-16 space-y-4 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
    <p
      class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400"
    >
      Architect / Data / Developer
    </p>
    <h1
      class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 sm:text-5xl"
    >
      {heroTitle}
    </h1>
    <p class="max-w-2xl text-lg text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      {heroSubtitle}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section
    id="about"
    aria-labelledby="about-heading"
    class="reading space-y-4 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300"
  >
    <h2
      id="about-heading"
      class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"
    >
      About
    </h2>
    <p>
      Hi, I'm Emmanuel on paper but Manny in person. I'm a developer at heart
      who started as a full-stack C# developer and discovered a passion for
      making sense of data. Over the years, I've gone from building web apps to
      architecting event-driven systems, to becoming a go-to for advice on data
      pipelines and shaping telemetry into meaningful insights.
    </p>
    <p>
      When I'm not architecting systems, you'll likely find me tinkering in my
      homelab or diving into creative projects that blend technology with
      everyday life.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section
    id="architecture"
    aria-labelledby="arch-heading"
    class="mt-12 reading space-y-4 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300"
  >
    <h2
      id="arch-heading"
      class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"
    >
      My Architecture Bias (Green-field Ideal)
    </h2>
    <p>
      This section is inspired by a paraphrased section in
      <a
        class="underline decoration-dashed underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-solid"
        href="https://a.co/d/fX3LOY6"
      >
        The Software Architect Elevator: Redefining the Architect's Role in the
        Digital Enterprise by Gregor Hohpe
      </a>
      .
    </p>
    <blockquote
      class="border-l-4 border-slate-300 pl-4 italic text-slate-600 dark:border-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300"
    >
      A software architect may have unconscious preferences or tendencies that
      impact their decision-making. Being aware of those biases can help you
      avoid blindly applying patterns that don't fit.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Being transparent about my tendencies can help the people I work with
      understand quickly where I may have blind spots. It's also important for
      me to stress that I am fully aware that every organization and project has
      its own unique constraints, and adopting my ideal architecture is almost
      never possible... but I still like to dream.
    </p>
    <p>
      My ideal architecture starts with web apps <DiagramIdentity symbol="W" /> as
      the primary front door. Behind that are HTTP-based APIs <DiagramIdentity
        symbol="H"
        family="violet"
      /> with self-generating open API specs. Under the hood, it's event-driven with
      a very strong streaming governance model. There is a clear decoupling between
      the product systems, analytical systems, and business systems.
    </p>

    <p id="gf-explanation">
      Create a few items through the Web app <DiagramIdentity symbol="W" />. The
      Product <DiagramIdentity symbol="P" /> saves each item and responds. Async Worker
      and Analytics <DiagramIdentity symbol="A" family="teal" />
      collect events into batches. “Pause after batch” lets Analytics finish its current
      batch, then stops it from starting another. New events keep queuing while Business
      <DiagramIdentity symbol="B" family="gold" /> continues. “Resume processing”
      lets Analytics catch up. That independence comes with stale observations.
    </p>
    <GreenfieldDiagram />
    <p>
      The <b>product systems</b>
      <DiagramIdentity symbol="P" /> have a goal to make both builders and operators
      happy: builders are developers (e.g., internal app devs or external devs) integrating
      via APIs/events, while operators (e.g., dispatchers or field workers) are application
      users who run the work without writing software. When a feature is built in
      the user interface, by default there is an equivalent public API for builders.
      The events they emit represent the key verbs you can perform against entities.
      This makes end users, analytical systems, and business systems happy.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <b>analytical systems</b>
      <DiagramIdentity symbol="A" family="teal" /> have a goal of combining and producing
      observations for the end users and internal stakeholders. They consume events
      and capture anything else they need via the product system's access-controlled
      API gateways, with privacy-aware handling of sensitive data. These observations
      are also emitted as events for other systems, typically sanitized and aggregated.
      It's important to note that it only makes sense for these systems to provide
      historical, merged or aggregated forms of data they consumed. One of the negative
      side effects of this architecture is the possibility of data staleness or inconsistency.
      Therefore an important part of their monitoring is building data quality checks,
      uniqueness checks, monitoring pipelines, and periodic checksum inspired validations.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <b>business systems</b>
      <DiagramIdentity symbol="B" family="gold" /> have a goal to make money. They
      focus on collecting the money, and finding more ways to keep that money coming
      in. Often, thats through upselling and cross-selling, marketing, and optimizing
      margins. From my experience, these systems are often third party systems CRMs
      and ERP systems. They integrate with the product systems via APIs and events
      and provide feedback loops. They also use the analytical systems to strategize
      and make data-driven decisions.
    </p>
    <p>More of this ideal will show up in a side project I'm working on.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
